# Valere core rules
## How to work
- Plan before code: for any change touching more than one file, first list the files to change, the approach, risks and test cases, then wait for approval.
- Work in small steps. After each step, stop and summarise what changed.
- Prefer existing code, helpers and patterns in this repo over new ones. Search before creating.
- Do not add new libraries unless the user explicitly asks. If one is needed, name it, explain why, and confirm it is widely used and maintained.
- If something is unclear or you are unsure, say so and ask. Never invent APIs, fields, env variables or business rules.
## Code quality
- Follow the stack rule and `project.mdc` for structure and naming.
- Small functions with clear names; no dead code, commented-out code, console logs or TODOs without a ticket ID.
- Handle errors explicitly; never swallow exceptions silently.
- Validate all external input (request bodies, query params, files, webhooks).
- Every new function with logic gets unit tests that cover the acceptance criteria and edge cases.
## Security (never break these)
- Never read, print, log, hard-code or commit secrets, tokens, passwords, keys or `.env` values. Use environment variables and the existing config module.
- Never use real customer or production data in code, tests or examples; use fake data.
- Never run commands that touch databases, cloud accounts, deployments, or that delete files, unless the user types the exact command.
- Use parameterised queries / ORM methods; never build SQL with string concatenation.
- Enforce authentication and authorisation checks on every new endpoint.
## Git
- Commit messages: Conventional Commits `type(scope): summary [TICKET]`, types feat, fix, refactor, perf, test, docs, chore, ci.
- Branches: `feature/VAL-123-short-desc`, `bugfix/...`, `hotfix/...`, lowercase with hyphens.
- Never suggest force-pushing to develop, release/* or main.


